    {"id": "CW-066", "language": "python", "description": "Implement an LRU cache using OrderedDict", "implementation": "from collections import OrderedDict\nclass LRU:\n    def __init__(self, capacity):\n        self.cap, self.cache = capacity, OrderedDict()\n    def get(self, key):\n        if key not in self.cache: return None\n        self.cache.move_to_end(key)\n        return self.cache[key]\n    def put(self, key, value):\n        if key in self.cache:\n            self.cache.move_to_end(key)\n        self.cache[key] = value\n        if len(self.cache) > self.cap:\n            self.cache.popitem(last=False)", "explanation": "OrderedDict preserves insertion order with O(1) move_to_end and popitem. get refreshes the recency by moving the key to the back; put on overflow drops the front (least-recently-used). functools.lru_cache wraps function calls with the same logic, but explicit caches need this pattern."},
    {"id": "CW-067", "language": "python", "description": "Implement quicksort recursively", "implementation": "def quicksort(arr):\n    if len(arr) < 2: return arr\n    pivot = arr[len(arr) // 2]\n    less = [x for x in arr if x < pivot]\n    equal = [x for x in arr if x == pivot]\n    greater = [x for x in arr if x > pivot]\n    return quicksort(less) + equal + quicksort(greater)", "explanation": "Three-way partition handles duplicates safely (no infinite recursion on equal elements). O(n log n) average, O(n^2) worst case (already-sorted input + naive pivot). Not in-place; uses O(n) extra space. For production, Python's built-in sorted() (Timsort) is always the right choice."},
    {"id": "CW-068", "language": "python", "description": "Find the shortest path in an unweighted graph", "implementation": "from collections import deque\ndef shortest_path(graph, start, end):\n    if start == end: return [start]\n    visited = {start}\n    parent = {}\n    q = deque([start])\n    while q:\n        node = q.popleft()\n        for n in graph[node]:\n            if n in visited: continue\n            visited.add(n)\n            parent[n] = node\n            if n == end:\n                path = [n]\n                while path[-1] != start:\n                    path.append(parent[path[-1]])\n                return list(reversed(path))\n            q.append(n)\n    return None", "explanation": "BFS gives shortest paths in unweighted graphs because nodes are visited in distance order. parent dict reconstructs the path from end back to start. Returns None if unreachable. For weighted graphs use Dijkstra (CE-058)."},
    {"id": "CW-069", "language": "python", "description": "Detect a cycle in a directed graph using DFS", "implementation": "def has_cycle(graph):\n    WHITE, GRAY, BLACK = 0, 1, 2\n    color = {n: WHITE for n in graph}\n    def dfs(u):\n        color[u] = GRAY\n        for v in graph[u]:\n            if color[v] == GRAY: return True\n            if color[v] == WHITE and dfs(v): return True\n        color[u] = BLACK\n        return False\n    return any(color[n] == WHITE and dfs(n) for n in graph)", "explanation": "Three colors mark progress: WHITE = unvisited, GRAY = on current DFS stack (in-progress), BLACK = fully processed. A back edge (GRAY child) means a cycle. O(V + E). For undirected graphs, just track parents and skip the immediate parent during DFS."},
